    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Chronic mechanical low back pain for about seven years of heavy training and fighting, aching across the lower back, worse after long drives and in the morning</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M54.59</td><td>S — Synthetic chronic back pain anchored to his documented back injuries during his career.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Lumbar disc herniation at L4-L5 with left L5 radiculopathy, a few weeks old and improving: sharp pain into the left buttock and calf after bending to lift a child at home, seen in a hospital emergency department, where MRI showed a left paracentral L4-L5 disc extrusion contacting the left L5 nerve root; leg pain now intermittent, with slight numbness over the top of the left foot and normal strength; no bladder or bowel change</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M51.16</td><td>S — Synthetic modern presenting problem anchored to his documented ruptured disc; the level, side and MRI findings are synthetic.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left thumb base (first carpometacarpal joint) after an old fracture at the base of the first metacarpal from his early fighting years, with pain on pinching and gripping, an ache after long gripping, and a positive grind test</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M18.32</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis anchored to his documented broken hands; which bones he broke is not recorded, and the left thumb is a synthetic choice.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Overweight after retirement, with a weight gain of about 9 kg in the four months since he stopped training, eating as he did in training camp; BMI 28.7</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E66.3</td><td>S — Synthetic weight change anchored to his documented heavy training and its sudden end at retirement.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Elevated blood pressure reading at intake (136/86 mmHg, average of two) without a diagnosis of hypertension; home readings to confirm</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R03.0</td><td>S — Synthetic finding after the end of heavy training; resolved with weight loss and exercise.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>History of repetitive head impacts from about thirteen years of Army, amateur and professional boxing, including heavy sparring; no remembered loss of consciousness; normal brain health baseline (no cognitive, mood or behavioral complaints; MoCA 29); traumatic encephalopathy syndrome not suspected</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z87.828</td><td>S — Synthetic risk-factor problem anchored to his documented career and sparring; no concussion or brain injury is documented in the sources read.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

