    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Lumbar disc herniation with left L5 radiculopathy (M51.16)</content>. Improving, with no motor deficit and no red flags. Dr. Achterberg-Lund agrees that no repeat imaging is needed and that surgery is not indicated unless weakness appears or disabling leg pain persists beyond six to twelve weeks. [S]<list><item>Physical therapy from this week, twice a week for six weeks.</item><item>Naproxen only when needed, with food.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Thumb-base osteoarthritis (M18.32)</content>. Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left first carpometacarpal joint; pain and weak pinch. [S]<list><item>Thumb orthosis fitted today for gripping work and at night.</item><item>Hand exercises from the hand therapist; continue the gel.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Elevated blood pressure reading (R03.0)</content>. Home average 126/78 mmHg: elevated blood pressure, not hypertension. Lifestyle change is the treatment. [S]<list><item>Less salt, regular aerobic exercise and weight loss.</item><item>Repeat a week of home readings before the twelve-week visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Repetitive head impacts, brain health baseline (Z87.828)</content>. Normal baseline: no cognitive, mood, behavioral, sleep or balance complaints, a normal neurological examination and MoCA 29. Dr. Achterberg-Lund explained that traumatic encephalopathy syndrome is a research diagnosis and that nothing now suggests it; no brain imaging is indicated. [S]<list><item>No sparring or other head impacts.</item><item>Repeat the baseline in a year, or sooner for any new symptom.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Back pain intensity (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5 out of 10, from 6</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Back function (Oswestry Disability Index)</td><td>At intake and every four to six weeks during treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>36% at intake; next at eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Leg neurological examination and red flags (weakness, saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change, fever, weight loss)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Straight-leg raise positive at 70 degrees on the left; full strength and normal reflexes; slight L5 sensory change, improving; no red flags</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Lumbar imaging</td><td>Only for red flags, a progressive deficit, or when surgery is considered</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The outside MRI from the emergency department was reviewed; no progressive deficit, so no repeat imaging</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy (physical therapy, then a home program)</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Physical therapy evaluation done after the consultation; twice a week for six weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral NSAID safety: dose and frequency actually taken, stomach symptoms, blood pressure, kidney function</td><td>Every visit while an oral NSAID is used</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naproxen twice a day for a week, now once a day with food; no stomach symptoms; blood pressure 130/82; creatinine 1.08 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>No opioids or muscle relaxants</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None prescribed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Thumb base pain (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5 out of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Hand function (FIHOA, recorded in the note) and grip strength</td><td>At diagnosis and with each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>FIHOA 9 out of 30; grip 38 kg left and 54 kg right. The hand function questionnaire was not given at intake; it was first given today, before the splint, so the baseline is still before treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Thumb joint examination (tenderness, grind, range, pinch)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tender carpometacarpal joint, positive grind, painful pinch</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Thumb orthosis and hand exercise program</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hand-based orthosis fitted today; hand exercises taught</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Topical diclofenac: use and skin</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three times a day; no skin irritation</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Hand X-ray</td><td>At diagnosis; not for routine follow-up</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Intake X-ray: first carpometacarpal narrowing and an old healed fracture at the base of the first metacarpal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Clinic blood pressure, average of two seated readings</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>130/82 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure monitoring (validated upper-arm cuff)</td><td>To confirm an elevated reading; then every three to six months while elevated</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven-day average 126/78 mmHg: elevated, not hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>91.6 kg, BMI 28.6</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Diet and physical activity (150 to 300 minutes a week)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking an hour a day; smaller portions; exercise prescription given today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel and 10-year risk (PREVENT)</td><td>At intake; repeat in four to six years at low risk</td><td>Up to date</td><td>LDL 137 mg/dL at intake; low 10-year risk at 32</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Brain health symptom inventory (headache, memory, attention, mood, behavior, sleep, balance) and neurological examination</td><td>Baseline, then yearly after a career with repetitive head impacts</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No complaints; normal cranial nerves, coordination, tandem gait and Romberg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognitive screen (MoCA)</td><td>Baseline, then yearly after a career with repetitive head impacts</td><td>Up to date</td><td>29 out of 30 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain imaging</td><td>Only for new neurological symptoms or signs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Normal baseline; no indication</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety screen (GAD-7)</td><td>At intake and when symptoms suggest it</td><td>Up to date</td><td>4 at intake, minimal</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Sports medicine consultation</td><td>At intake</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Achterberg-Lund today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Leg pain gone and back pain at 3 or less out of 10, with an Oswestry index under 20%. In progress; target 2026-09-29 [S]</item><item>Grip and pinch without thumb pain at 3 or less out of 10. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Lose 5% to 7% of body weight over six months with regular exercise. In progress; target 2027-02-04 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure under 120/80 mmHg at home without medicine. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Keep a documented brain health baseline and repeat it yearly. In progress; target 2027-08-04 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis/osteoarthritis, hypertension and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for Traumatic Encephalopathy Syndrome (2021); 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); 2026 ACC/AHA/AACVPR/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Dyslipidemia (2026); Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans, 2nd edition (2018); Anxiety Disorders in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Physical therapy twice a week from this week. Preventive visit in three weeks. Eight-week review at the end of September. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-08: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

